different types of time complexity: 
                1. Constant Time Complexity         (O(1))      : The running time of the algorithm remains constant regardless of the input size.
                2. Logarithmic Time Complexity      (O(log n))  : The running time grows logarithmically with the input size, often seen in algorithms that divide the problem in half at each step.
                3. Linear Time Complexity           (O(n))      : The running time grows linearly with the input size, meaning it increases proportionally to the number of elements in the input.
                4. Linearithmic Time Complexity     (O(n log n)): The running time grows in proportion to n log n, commonly found in efficient sorting algorithms like mergesort and heapsort.
                5. Quadratic Time Complexity        (O(n^2))    : The running time grows quadratically with the input size, often seen in algorithms with nested loops over the input data.
                6. Cubic Time Complexity            (O(n^3))    : The running time grows cubically with the input size, typically found in algorithms with three nested loops.
                7. Exponential Time Complexity      (O(2^n))    : The running time doubles with each additional element in the input, often seen in brute-force algorithms for combinatorial problems.
                8. Factorial Time Complexity        (O(n!))     : The running time grows factorially with the input size, usually associated with algorithms that generate all permutations of a set.

these are the example and the explanations of each type of time complexity.

HOW TO FIND THE big O notation of an algorithm:

f(x) = 3x^2 + 2x + 1

        = O(3x^2) + O(2x) + O(1)
        = O(x^2) + O(x) + O(1)
        =O(x^2) + O(x)      //  x^2  >   x  > 1
        = O(x^2)

